{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a count of Tripoli who ruled from 1101 to 1130. He was a prominent figure in the Crusades and a key leader in the defense of the County of Tripoli against various Muslim powers. His reign saw significant military campaigns and political maneuvering shaping the region's power dynamics.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension*\n\nRaymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1101. His early years were marked by consolidating his power and securing the county's borders. He faced challenges from both internal factions and external threats particularly from the Seljuk Turks. Raymond skillfully navigated these conflicts establishing alliances and implementing military reforms. He was known for his piety and his commitment to Christian defense. He also focused on strengthening the county's infrastructure and economy.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*\n\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by numerous military campaigns. He engaged in several conflicts with the Seljuk Turks who exerted considerable pressure on Tripoli. These conflicts often involved sieges battles and diplomatic negotiations. He also fought against other Muslim dynasties and local Berber tribes. Raymond's military successes helped maintain the County of Tripoli's independence and solidify its position as a significant power in the Levant. He was known for his strategic thinking and his ability to inspire his troops. He also invested heavily in building fortifications to protect the county. He successfully defended Tripoli from multiple sieges demonstrating his military prowess.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III's reign is considered a pivotal period in the history of the County of Tripoli. He successfully defended the county from numerous threats and established a period of relative stability and prosperity. H